      "slug": "new-town-vs-salt-lake-where-to-buy-in-2026",
      "title": "New Town vs Salt Lake: where should a first-time buyer look in 2026?",
      "date": "2026-11-01",
      "author": "HouseRoot team",
      "cover": "",
      "excerpt": "Metro access, school density, flooding history and price per sq ft, compared block by block for the two localities buyers ask us about most.",
      "body": "## The short answer\n\nIf you need possession within a year, Salt Lake's resale stock wins. If you can wait and want a newer building with amenities, New Town's Action Area II and III launches are better value per sq ft.\n\n## What we compared\n\n- **Metro**: walking distance to the nearest operational station\n- **Schools**: count within 3 km\n- **Water-logging**: history over the last five monsoons\n- **Price**: carpet-area price per sq ft on the last ten deals we closed\n\n> We're paid by the developer, so the price you pay is the same as walking in directly. Our verdict is still ours.\n\n*Replace this sample post from the admin panel.*",
      "published": true
    },
    {
      "id": "b2",
      "slug": "what-rera-registration-actually-protects",
      "title": "What RERA registration actually protects you from (and what it doesn't)",
      "date": "2026-10-20",
      "author": "HouseRoot team",
      "cover": "",
      "excerpt": "A registration number is not a guarantee. Here is what to check on the WBRERA portal before you pay a booking amount.",
      "body": "## What it covers\n\n1. The project must deposit 70% of collections in a separate account\n2. Carpet area has a legal definition\n3. Delay compensation is enforceable\n\n## What it does not cover\n\nConstruction quality, the developer's track record on earlier projects, or whether the approach road is actually built. That is what a site visit is for.\n\n*Replace this sample post from the admin panel.*",
